ram {*} x\n */\nMyQueue.prototype.push = function (x) {\n  // TODO: implement\n};\n\n/**\n */\nMyQueue.prototype.pop = function () {\n  // TODO: implement\n};\n\n/**\n */\nMyQueue.prototype.peek = function () {\n  // TODO: implement\n};\n\n/**\n */\nMyQueue.prototype.empty = function () {\n  // TODO: implement\n};\n"},"editorial":{"explanation":"Two stacks. Everything pushed goes onto the inbox; everything read comes off the outbox.\n\nThe outbox is only refilled when it runs dry, and refilling reverses the order — which is exactly\nwhat turns LIFO into FIFO. Each element moves between the stacks at most once, so the amortised cost\nper operation is `O(1)` even though a single `pop` can be `O(n)`.\n\nRefilling on every operation instead is the version that degrades to `O(n)`.","code":{"python":"class MyQueue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.inbox = []\n        self.outbox = []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.inbox.append(x)\n\n    def _shift(self):\n        if not self.outbox:\n            while self.inbox:\n                self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())\n\n    def pop(self):\n        self._shift()\n        return self.outbox.pop()\n\n    def peek(self):\n        self._shift()\n        return self.outbox[-1]\n\n    def empty(self):\n        return not self.inbox and not self.outbox\n"},"complexity":"O(1) amortised per operation"}}
